WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.423
A Cloud PKI root needs
no existing private PKI,

2
00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:08.749
brings a new chain that has to reach
every relying party and every forest,

3
00:00:08.749 --> 00:00:12.300
and keeps the root key in
a Microsoft-managed HSM.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:18.343
BYOCA needs a private CA to sign, preserves
the chain relying parties already hold,

5
00:00:18.343 --> 00:00:24.386
keeps root custody wherever your AD CS root
key is, and adds one ongoing on-premises

6
00:00:24.386 --> 00:00:30.163
dependency: the private root's CRL must
stay published and reachable over HTTP.

7
00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:35.284
Neither model can serve domain
controller, LDAPS or server certificates,

8
00:00:35.284 --> 00:00:39.313
because Cloud PKI issues only
to Intune-enrolled devices.

9
00:00:39.313 --> 00:00:41.847
And rollback is simpler under BYOCA,

10
00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:45.987
because the original root and
its issuing CAs keep running.

11
00:00:45.987 --> 00:00:51.056
The program's recommendation, for
an organization with an established

12
00:00:51.056 --> 00:00:55.524
AD CS hierarchy, an offline root
and cross-forest trust, is BYOCA.

13
00:00:55.524 --> 00:00:59.742
It preserves the trust chain,
needs one cloud CA rather than two,

14
00:00:59.742 --> 00:01:02.012
and keeps a natural rollback path.

15
00:01:02.012 --> 00:01:08.093
For an organization building PKI for the
first time alongside cloud-first identity,

16
00:01:08.093 --> 00:01:12.001
a Cloud PKI root removes
every on-premises dependency.

17
00:01:12.001 --> 00:01:14.650
Three program rules go with BYOCA.

18
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.890
Harden first: the root's
hierarchy passes the

19
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.480
Chapter 03 gate before it
signs a cloud issuing CA.

20
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.033
Sign with the root, not an AD CS
issuing CA, so the cloud issuer is a

21
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:31.287
peer of the existing issuing CAs and
retiring them later doesn't touch it.

22
00:01:31.287 --> 00:01:33.818
And treat the root CRL as production:

23
00:01:33.818 --> 00:01:39.134
publish it to an internet-reachable HTTP
location on a schedule with margin,

24
00:01:39.134 --> 00:01:44.520
monitor it, and record the next ceremony
date before the current CRL expires.
